1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,320
Close the door of your room.

2
00:00:01,550 --> 00:00:02,860
Shut the windows.

3
00:00:03,090 --> 00:00:06,260
The air inside stays inside.

4
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:09,980
It doesn't seep away
in a direction you can't point to.

5
00:00:10,170 --> 00:00:12,290
That sounds too obvious to say.

6
00:00:13,010 --> 00:00:14,390
But last time, we imagined

7
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:17,330
a fourth direction of space,
one that would let you

8
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:20,130
reach into a locked safe
without opening it.

9
00:00:20,357 --> 00:00:23,977
So if that direction is real,
why isn't everything leaking into it?

10
00:00:24,437 --> 00:00:28,937
The air from your room.
The heat from your coffee. You.

11
00:00:29,505 --> 00:00:31,725
Part of the answer, we already have.

12
00:00:32,405 --> 00:00:35,945
A big, open fourth dimension
would wreck the solar system.

13
00:00:36,445 --> 00:00:38,485
Planets couldn't hold their orbits.

14
00:00:38,926 --> 00:00:41,466
So if there's one at all,
it has to be hidden.

15
00:00:42,266 --> 00:00:46,686
Curled up too small to notice, or out of
reach, because we're stuck to our three.

16
00:00:47,901 --> 00:00:51,741
And for the second idea, there
may be a clue hanging on your fridge.

17
00:00:52,435 --> 00:00:55,115
A small magnet holds up a sheet of paper.

18
00:00:55,735 --> 00:00:59,275
Pulling down on that paper
is the gravity of the entire Earth.

19
00:00:59,895 --> 00:01:01,175
And the magnet wins.

20
00:01:01,964 --> 00:01:03,664
Take two protons.

21
00:01:04,244 --> 00:01:06,824
The electric push between them
is stronger than their gravity

22
00:01:07,054 --> 00:01:09,864
by about a trillion
trillion trillion times.

23
00:01:10,681 --> 00:01:13,241
Physicists call this
the hierarchy problem.

24
00:01:13,621 --> 00:01:16,101
Why is gravity so absurdly weak?

25
00:01:16,441 --> 00:01:17,741
Nobody knows.

26
00:01:18,525 --> 00:01:22,605
In 1998, three physicists
offered a startling answer.

27
00:01:23,385 --> 00:01:25,425
Maybe gravity isn't weak at all.

28
00:01:25,865 --> 00:01:27,905
Maybe it only looks weak to us.

29
00:01:28,702 --> 00:01:31,962
Picture our universe
as ink on a page, like last time.

30
00:01:32,622 --> 00:01:35,342
Atoms, light and people
are stuck to the page.

31
00:01:36,002 --> 00:01:37,282
Gravity isn't.

32
00:01:37,805 --> 00:01:40,505
It spreads into the space
around the page as well.

33
00:01:41,285 --> 00:01:43,545
So by the time it reaches us,
it's diluted.

34
00:01:44,185 --> 00:01:48,385
It feels feeble, because it's shared
with directions we can't see.

35
00:01:49,177 --> 00:01:52,657
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

36
00:01:53,277 --> 00:01:54,897
It really helps the channel.

37
00:01:57,067 --> 00:02:01,527
It's only an idea. But it's a useful one,
because it makes predictions.

38
00:02:02,367 --> 00:02:04,087
And predictions can be checked.

39
00:02:04,547 --> 00:02:06,267
And that gives us a test.

40
00:02:06,867 --> 00:02:09,627
Far apart, gravity follows the old rule.

41
00:02:10,087 --> 00:02:12,787
Double the distance,
and it gets four times weaker.

42
00:02:13,243 --> 00:02:17,063
But bring two objects closer
than the size of those hidden dimensions,

43
00:02:17,503 --> 00:02:20,523
and gravity would suddenly
grow stronger than it should.

44
00:02:21,083 --> 00:02:24,443
In 1998, nobody could rule it out,

45
00:02:24,523 --> 00:02:27,823
because nobody had measured gravity
at distances that small.

46
00:02:28,496 --> 00:02:30,256
It's brutally hard to do.

47
00:02:30,676 --> 00:02:34,196
At that scale, gravity is drowned out
by almost everything else.

48
00:02:34,756 --> 00:02:37,816
Stray electric charges. Vibration.

49
00:02:38,316 --> 00:02:39,816
A change in temperature.

50
00:02:40,609 --> 00:02:42,969
In Seattle, a team
at the University of Washington

51
00:02:43,199 --> 00:02:47,809
hangs a thin disc from a wire,
and turns another disc beneath it.

52
00:02:48,509 --> 00:02:50,809
If gravity changed at short range,

53
00:02:50,889 --> 00:02:53,609
the hanging disc would twist
in a telltale rhythm.

54
00:02:54,189 --> 00:02:57,969
In 2020, they reported
that gravity still follows the old rule

55
00:02:58,199 --> 00:03:00,642
down to fifty-two micrometres.

56
00:03:00,909 --> 00:03:03,029
That's thinner than a human hair.

57
00:03:03,474 --> 00:03:07,894
So any hidden dimensions that gravity
leaks into must be smaller than that.

58
00:03:08,494 --> 00:03:13,514
And the version from 1998 that needed
them a millimetre wide is ruled out.

59
00:03:15,994 --> 00:03:18,674
If gravity really is strong
at tiny distances,

60
00:03:19,074 --> 00:03:23,514
then smashing particles together
hard enough could let gravity take over.

61
00:03:24,314 --> 00:03:27,654
Theory said the result
could be a microscopic black hole.

62
00:03:28,252 --> 00:03:31,092
At the Large Hadron Collider,
physicists went looking

63
00:03:31,322 --> 00:03:33,372
for those black holes on purpose.

64
00:03:34,172 --> 00:03:37,612
Finding one would have meant
gravity leaks into hidden dimensions.

65
00:03:38,526 --> 00:03:40,686
They also looked for something subtler.

66
00:03:40,766 --> 00:03:43,926
A collision where some energy
seems to vanish,

67
00:03:44,006 --> 00:03:47,246
as if something had slipped
off the page and carried it away.

68
00:03:47,704 --> 00:03:50,964
But missing energy isn't a photograph
of another dimension.

69
00:03:52,024 --> 00:03:55,944
Ghostly particles called neutrinos
slip out of the detector too.

70
00:03:56,024 --> 00:04:00,884
So every event like that has to be checked
against everything we already know.

71
00:04:01,457 --> 00:04:04,677
So far, the books balance. No black holes.

72
00:04:05,257 --> 00:04:08,337
No missing energy
beyond what known physics explains.

73
00:04:10,867 --> 00:04:14,847
The third test came from a hundred
and thirty million light-years away.

74
00:04:15,847 --> 00:04:20,847
In August 2017, detectors on Earth
picked up a ripple in space itself.

75
00:04:21,483 --> 00:04:24,763
Two neutron stars,
the collapsed cores of dead stars,

76
00:04:24,843 --> 00:04:27,203
had spiralled into each other and merged.

77
00:04:27,963 --> 00:04:31,223
Less than two seconds later,
a space telescope caught

78
00:04:31,453 --> 00:04:34,343
a burst of gamma rays
from the same patch of sky.

79
00:04:35,063 --> 00:04:37,223
For the first time,
the same event had been seen

80
00:04:37,453 --> 00:04:40,103
in gravitational waves and in light.

81
00:04:41,003 --> 00:04:43,943
And that let physicists ask
a very simple question.

82
00:04:44,569 --> 00:04:47,769
If gravity leaked into
extra dimensions along the way,

83
00:04:47,849 --> 00:04:50,409
the ripple would arrive
fainter than it should.

84
00:04:51,269 --> 00:04:55,569
The galaxy would seem further away
in gravitational waves than in light.

85
00:04:56,230 --> 00:04:57,450
It didn't.

86
00:04:57,597 --> 00:04:59,297
The two distances agreed.

87
00:05:00,296 --> 00:05:02,976
Across a hundred and thirty
million light-years,

88
00:05:03,116 --> 00:05:06,016
the ripple faded just as it would
if gravity had only our three

89
00:05:06,246 --> 00:05:08,056
dimensions to spread through.

90
00:05:08,536 --> 00:05:11,316
If any of it leaked,
it was too little to see.

91
00:05:14,116 --> 00:05:15,256
So here's the scorecard.

92
00:05:15,486 --> 00:05:17,196
On a lab bench, no leak.

93
00:05:17,426 --> 00:05:19,256
In the collider, no leak.

94
00:05:19,486 --> 00:05:21,816
Across the universe, no leak.

95
00:05:22,409 --> 00:05:24,769
That doesn't mean
there's no fourth dimension.

96
00:05:25,729 --> 00:05:28,049
It means the hiding places
we've checked are empty,

97
00:05:28,279 --> 00:05:31,209
and the ones left are smaller,
or stranger.

98
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:35,140
The oldest hiding place is still open.

99
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:39,640
A dimension curled up
far smaller than any experiment can reach.

100
00:05:40,357 --> 00:05:42,577
Even then, it might leave a trace.

101
00:05:43,537 --> 00:05:46,797
In some theories,
a particle moving around that tiny circle

102
00:05:47,027 --> 00:05:50,357
would show up to us as a heavier copy
of a familiar particle.

103
00:05:50,821 --> 00:05:54,961
That doesn't mean an electron
is secretly a tiny four-dimensional ball.

104
00:05:55,801 --> 00:05:59,181
It's a specific prediction:
new particles, at particular masses.

105
00:05:59,411 --> 00:06:01,861
None has been found.

106
00:06:02,569 --> 00:06:06,489
String theory offers one reason
physicists still take this seriously.

107
00:06:07,449 --> 00:06:10,449
Its consistent versions
require extra dimensions.

108
00:06:11,129 --> 00:06:15,149
But a mathematical requirement
is not evidence that they're real.

109
00:06:17,869 --> 00:06:19,909
Remember the square in Flatland.

110
00:06:20,549 --> 00:06:24,029
He learned about the third dimension
because a sphere came to visit.

111
00:06:24,910 --> 00:06:28,410
We're not waiting for a visitor.
We're looking for footprints.

112
00:06:29,010 --> 00:06:31,790
A pull that's slightly too strong
at the width of a hair.

113
00:06:32,270 --> 00:06:34,130
A collision that doesn't add up.

114
00:06:34,630 --> 00:06:36,670
A ripple that arrives too faint.

115
00:06:37,441 --> 00:06:41,041
Meanwhile, the magnet on your fridge
is still beating the whole planet.

116
00:06:41,681 --> 00:06:43,301
And nobody knows why.

117
00:06:44,275 --> 00:06:46,055
So the question has changed.

118
00:06:46,735 --> 00:06:49,675
It's no longer what another dimension
would look like.

119
00:06:49,975 --> 00:06:52,565
It's what it could do
that would finally give it away.
